A||N||12||Will||Muffler for Northstar (esp. 1FST2M6, RogerGarrison, and Northstar owners :) )||||1||
Z||000000||Will||07-09-2001||10:37 PM||||I finally have my Northstar project underway.<BR>I'm doing machining on the trans case this week, and I just ordered my clutch and torsen.<P>I'm thinking over my exhaust options:<BR>I was thinking of a system like the V6 Fiero's, only in 3 inch pipe. I will run a cat, so please don't tell me to not run a cat. The entire system will also be stainless steel.<BR>I'm still torn between wanting V8 rumble or Ferrari-esque shriek.<BR>The important thing I want is to not compromise power.<P>1FST2M6:<BR>     I noticed in a picture I saw of the Cheeto that it had one big exhaust outlet on the left side. I've heard you say that a single-in/single-out setup flows better than the stock Fiero dual-in/dual-out setup. How much better? You don't seem like to type to do something unless it works, so I'm guessing it is worth having the lopsided looking rear end. Is 3" pipe enough for 300 HP?<P>RogerGarrison:<BR>     You said once that the exhaust on your 3.1 turbo 308 sounds a lot like a Ferrari... what setup are you using?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Slammed Fiero||07-09-2001||10:44 PM||||wanna buy a dual in Single out B&B triflow system for a 911??? All stainless steel construction.<P>I can get pics and measurements this weekend. Worth about $1200 New.<P>This put down 19more rear wheel HP over the stock 911 system. It has a tiny cat converter , the size of a coke can.<P>I will get exact measurements of the muffler if your interested and pics this weekend. We swapped the system out when we went to a bigger motor. The system fed a 340hp 911 that revved to 8 grand , so no doubt it will feed a N*<P>Just an idea for you , the muffler is a work of art , the body of it is very similar in size to a factory fiero muffler<P>JM <P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Will||07-09-2001||11:21 PM||||Slammed:<BR>Yes, please post pics and measurements.<BR>How much are you selling it for?<P><BR>More generally:<BR>What about dual-in/single-out cats, say with 2 or 2.5" inlets and 3" outlet? Do they exist? If so, who sells and for how much?<P>I'd like that setup because the volume of the cat would decouple the resonant properties of one bank from the other.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||07-10-2001||01:16 AM||||mine uses stock manifolds. turbo is built onto crossover pipe OEM diameter. goes thru cat and straight down in center. muffler has center inlet and splits into a Y to exit at both sides. Im sure some of the sound (the Ferrari wail) is due to the turbo too, and I do like to rev it.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||1FST2M6||07-10-2001||08:24 AM||||the single in dual out had a bad flat spot in the torque curve .. in both the ocelot and 50 series flowmaster. i used a 40 series single in single out and picked up a few hp/tq.  the brullen unit i have now is a 2.5" single in/out. which picked up 6 or so hp over stock. the single in/out keeps the air flowing and allows the exhaust pulses to help push itself out. where the single in dual aout will bounce around and the flow slows down considerably. if you get the chance look at borlas web site <A HREF="http://www.borla.com" TARGET=_blank>www.borla.com</A>  and see how they have their camaro/firebird exhausts.. this would be what you want.. since your dicplacement is so much more than normal. the single in single out with a rectrictor plate to tune the noise to your liking and performance needs. the Y-pipe can be picked up at any decent muffler shop. and usually runs about $60 with the flanges (but not the plates) the muffler   <A HREF="http://www.brullenexhaust.com" TARGET=_blank>www.brullenexhaust.com</A>  or <A HREF="http://www.powertone.com" TARGET=_blank>www.powertone.com</A>  i'm all about function.. i didn't really car aobut balanced looks. where you with a N* probly will want both.. the cut out/restrictor is probly what you'll want to look into. not a good idea on a V6,they tend to loose to much torque with and open enxhaust which cuts in to daily drivability.  <P>a B&B unit is a diamond of a differnt cut...  if you can get your hands on a B&B single in dual out get it! they are able to support a STOOPID high flow rate even with thir x-flow design. <A HREF="http://www.bbtriflo.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.bbtriflo.com/</A>   i had a B&B on a 300zx TT and it was AWSOME! not as pricey as the porsche unit but still nicer than Greddy or HKS<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 07-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Slammed Fiero||07-10-2001||01:43 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/exhaust_1.jpg"> <P>Ok here is a pic of the exhaust that REPLACED the one I am offering to you. Dimension wise it's identical. This is just a pic to tide you over until the weekend when I can get more specifics. <P>I was going to swap the muffler into my car , but I thought a muffler worth more than my motor (2.8) wasn't worth it really , plus I plan to do an engine swap to my car in the next year (this winter) so I don't want to mess around it<P>As for the price , I will have to ask my dad , it's not mine. But it is just sitting there in the corner of the garage , along with 1000 other performance parts...from 2 aluminum block buick v8's , an intercooled 2.9ltr flat six corvair motor looking for a home, you name it , I dig hard enough , it's probably there.<P>This setup was designed for use with B&B headers , so the inlets are just universal flanges. All stainless steel , really nice.<P>I talked to my exhaust guy and he said it could fit a fiero with a little work , the big issue is the muffler body , which I know will fit a fiero.<P>The one in the pic was custom made for the new motor. I got a tour of B&B's facility..nothing short of amazing<P>JM<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Will||07-10-2001||03:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B> <P>Ok here is a pic of the exhaust that REPLACED the one I am offering to you. Dimension wise it's identical. This is just a pic to tide you over until the weekend when I can get more specifics. <P>I was going to swap the muffler into my car , but I thought a muffler worth more than my motor (2.8) wasn't worth it really , plus I plan to do an engine swap to my car in the next year (this winter) so I don't want to mess around it<P>As for the price , I will have to ask my dad , it's not mine. But it is just sitting there in the corner of the garage , along with 1000 other performance parts...from 2 aluminum block buick v8's , an intercooled 2.9ltr flat six corvair motor looking for a home, you name it , I dig hard enough , it's probably there.<P>This setup was designed for use with B&B headers , so the inlets are just universal flanges. All stainless steel , really nice.<P>I talked to my exhaust guy and he said it could fit a fiero with a little work , the big issue is the muffler body , which I know will fit a fiero.<P>The one in the pic was custom made for the new motor. I got a tour of B&B's facility..nothing short of amazing<P>JM<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Are you suggesting that I use this system as advertised and figure out how to build a dual-in/single-out exhaust around it, or would it work well if I used it "backwards" as a single-in/dual-out setup?<P>What about the sound? How loud will it be? I'd rather not have the whole county (yes, I'm from out in the stix where we still think of counties) know when I drop the hammer.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>What you've said so far sounds great. Keep those pics coming.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Will||07-10-2001||03:42 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>mine uses stock manifolds. turbo is built onto crossover pipe OEM diameter. goes thru cat and straight down in center. muffler has center inlet and splits into a Y to exit at both sides. Im sure some of the sound (the Ferrari wail) is due to the turbo too, and I do like to rev it.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What I'm visualizing is that your entire exhaust is behind the engine: the turbo is mounted on crossover like Dennis Lagrua's, but with the turbine outlet pointed aft, so that your cat is above your rear axle and your mufflers in approximately the stock location. Is that right?<P>What kind of mufflers are you using?<P>I'd like to work an exhaust that short, but the best way to do it would use the stock Cadillac manifolds, and then I wouldn't be able to fit in a cat.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Slammed Fiero||07-10-2001||03:44 PM||||Right now it's a dual in single out unit. The muffler sits upright , not as shon in the picture , where it lies flat. (like a fiero system)<P>I will have more pics this weekend for you , as well as complete measurements.<P>There are 2 tips with the system. The one has a cat converter , the other is a "track tip"<P>as for the sound. I won't lie ..she ain't quiet. but street legal..<P>I wish I had a recording of the system on the 911. IT is loud , but not obtrusive. it didn't have any highway drones at certian rpm's like a lot of systems.<P>Keep in mind this system was used on a RSR 911. Ie the car has NO insulation or sound deadening, no carpet , nothing. I have ridden in the car on hour trips and had no problems with the sound. My mom has taken a few trips too int he car down to NYS with my father and hasd not complained about the sound. So I doubt it would be nearly as loud on a N*<P>JM <p>[This message has been edited by Slammed Fiero (edited 07-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Will||07-10-2001||04:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B>Right now it's a dual in single out unit. The muffler sits upright , not as shon in the picture , where it lies flat. (like a fiero system)<P>I will have more pics this weekend for you , as well as complete measurements.<P>There are 2 tips with the system. The one has a cat converter , the other is a "track tip"<P>as for the sound. I won't lie ..she ain't quiet. but street legal..<P>I wish I had a recording of the system on the 911. IT is loud , but not obtrusive. it didn't have any highway drones at certian rpm's like a lot of systems.<P>Keep in mind this system was used on a RSR 911. Ie the car has NO insulation or sound deadening, no carpet , nothing. I have ridden in the car on hour trips and had no problems with the sound. My mom has taken a few trips too int he car down to NYS with my father and hasd not complained about the sound. So I doubt it would be nearly as loud on a N*<P>JM <P>[This message has been edited by Slammed Fiero (edited 07-10-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Waht do you mean "right now"? Can it be reconfigured to use as a single-in/dual-out? Would it be as effective? <P>A dual-in/single-out configuration is workable, and might even let me use more stock Cadillac parts than the system I had originally envisioned, but that's not as big a cat as I had hoped to use. Oh well... The environment can take one car's smog, right?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I bet having the cat after the muffler doesn't do a thing for lightoff time, though.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Slammed Fiero||07-10-2001||04:37 PM||||The Northstar is a fairly enviromentally friendly motor in comparison to whats in the 911. The 911 passed emmissions with that little cat converter. <P>As for modifying the exhuast. I don't want to BS you and say yeah! cut it open.<P>I would email B&B triflow and ask them<P>The system is from a 94 C2 911 RS America. it is the system setup for their headers. <P>I will get back to you as soon as I get pics of the system with measurements.<P>JM <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||rogergarrison||07-10-2001||04:50 PM||||the exhaust outlet of turbo (just below and left of Ign coil) faces to the right, goes directly into cat (in center of car pointing downward) the Y after the cat is also the mufflers, they both go towards each side with just a chrome dual tip. you can see just the sides of tips under the rear quarter panels in pic.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Jay||07-11-2001||12:38 AM||||Will my Northstar retains the stock manifolds and is kept as a true duel with 3" stainless piping.  Where the manifolds meet side by side I've got two cats (one for each bank) and then one elbows left the other  right.  The sound is quiet at idle but howls when pushed.  No mufflers just the two cats.||||reg||1
